  capability, which is \E[H\E[2J with no E3. The extra sequence comes
  from the binary, not the terminal database. So the command now
  agrees with the keystroke, and emits those bytes from a shell
  builtin (~30us) rather than a fork (~500us).

  The point is your history, not the microseconds: scroll back with
  your terminal's usual keys and the output that used to be thrown
  away is still there.
HELP
      ;;
    port)
      cat <<'HELP'
port — check what's using a port

  port 3000         Show process on port 3000
  port 8080         Show process on port 8080

  Tries lsof first, falls back to
  ss -tlnp. Shows PID, process name, and state.
HELP
      ;;
    killport)
      cat <<'HELP'
killport - kill the process listening on a port

  killport 3000     Stop whatever is serving on :3000
  killport 8080

  Sends TERM, so the server runs its shutdown handlers. Nothing
  survives that? fuser -k -KILL -n tcp 3000

  Only LISTENING sockets, never outbound connections -- `killport 443`
  will not touch your browser. (`lsof -i :443` would: it matches the
  port at either end. That is why this is fuser and not lsof.)

  Ports below 1024 usually belong to root, and the restriction is on
  bind(), not on kill -- so killport reports nothing and exits 1
  rather than failing loudly. Confirm with `port <num>`, then:
    sudo fuser -k -TERM -n tcp <num>

  Linux only (fuser is psmisc, which reads /proc).
HELP
      ;;
    dots)
      cat <<'HELP'
dots — the dotfiles console

  A tree of everything this repo can do on the left, what the thing
  under the cursor actually IS on the right.

  The point is not saved keystrokes — `make status` is nine characters.
  It is not having to already know that `make status` exists, what it
  answers, and how it differs from `make check`.

  Three kinds of row:

    targets     Every target `make help` lists. Enter runs it on the
                REAL terminal, so sudo can prompt and the output is
                exactly what you would have seen typing the command.
    sections    Every section in deps.conf. Enter OPENS it rather than
                running anything: a section is an argument, not a
                target — `make nvim` is a no-op stub — so the three
                verbs that do take one are rows underneath.
                SPACE toggles whether this machine sweeps a section,
                writing ~/.config/dotfiles/sections. Not deps.conf,
                which every machine reads — the skip stays outside the
                work tree, so it never becomes a diff you carry.
    theme       Opens `font`'s own picker, fonts and colors. Not a copy
                of it: the same screen its binary runs, pushed onto
                this one.

  Watch the sections header, because the two states differ in a way
  a row of ticks cannot show:

    following deps.conf    no file. Every section, including ones
                           added to deps.conf later.
    N of M on this machine a file exists. Exactly these names, so a
                           section added later arrives OFF.

  The `follow deps.conf` row goes back to the first state by deleting
  the file. Ticking everything is NOT the same thing.

  Either way `make install <section>` still installs it: naming a
  section explicitly wins, which is what makes this a preference
  rather than a wall.

  Nothing in it is authored twice. Target summaries are the `##` text
  `make help` already shows; the long form of a target is the comment
  block above its rule in the Makefile; sections come from deps.conf.
  Which means a comment above a rule is user-facing documentation now
  — write it for someone deciding whether to run the thing.

  It is not a make target, on purpose. `make font` does not exist
  either, and a second name for one thing is drift. `make help` points
  at it so it is still discoverable from the place you look when lost.

  in the console: j/k move · h/l close/open · gg/G ends
                  ctrl-d/u page · enter run or open · space toggle
                  a section on this machine · q quit
HELP
      ;;
    font)
      cat <<'HELP'
font — pick the font and the colors for each of wezterm's lanes

  font              Open the picker: a Fonts tab and a Colors tab
  font show         What is set, and whether wezterm is honoring it
  font reset        Back to the defaults wezterm.lua and nvim ship

  `font show` ends with the CARRIER — real SGR 6 and SGR 5 text next to
  plain. Three different faces means the lanes are live; one face means
  wezterm is not matching font_rules on the blink attribute and every
  lane is silently rendering in the base font. `make status wezterm`
  runs the same thing alongside the checks a program can make.

  That is the whole command surface. Everything else is inside the TUI,
  which states its own keys in the footer — including browsing Google's
  fonts, which is the last row under an opened lane.

  Colors are the second tab: k off the top row reaches the tabs. The
  shell lane takes one of the themes in wezterm/themes and an opacity;
  nvim's two lanes take any colorscheme nvim has installed, transparent
  or with its own background. A pick applies at once, in the terminal
  and in every nvim that is running. `claude` has no colors of its own:
  wezterm colors a window, not a pane.

  Every pick is one line in ~/.config/dotfiles/theme.conf, outside the
  work tree, so a pull cannot touch it.

  The lanes:

    shell        The shell, and any pane that is neither nvim nor Claude.
    nvim.ui      nvim's chrome. NOT just oil — oil is the most visible
                 thing it draws, but the lane is the window's base font
                 while nvim holds focus, so it is also telescope, the
                 statusline, the gutter and every float.
    nvim.editor  The file you are editing, and nothing else on screen.
    claude       Claude Code panes, pinned so a shell pick can't drag
                 them along.

  Why "lanes" and not just "the font":

    A font in wezterm is a property of the WINDOW or a property of the
    CELL, and there is nothing in between — no per-pane font exists.

    shell, nvim.ui and claude are the window lane, resolved from whichever
    pane has FOCUS. So tabs are exact and splits are not: two panes side
    by side share one font, the focused pane's.

    nvim.editor is the cell lane, and that is what makes "the file but not
    oil" possible at all. wezterm pins the blink rate to zero, which frees
    the blink attribute to mean "draw this in the other font"; nvim emits
    it on real file buffers and nowhere else. Being per-cell, it holds in
    an unfocused split too.

  What the numbers in the pane mean, since the screen shows them but
  cannot explain them:

    space     Width AND line height. Line height is the one nobody
              guesses: it is set by the font, not the terminal, and it
              ranges from 1.13em to 1.56em across the families here, so
              it decides how many rows you get.
    glyphs    How far apart 0/O, 1/l and l/I actually render, measured by
              rasterising the pair and diffing it. Higher is more
              distinct. The pairs you pick a coding font to keep apart.
    cuts      A font_rule replaces the face, so a family with no Italic
              takes the slant off every comment rose-pine italicises.
    claude    Claude Code's TUI glyphs in tiers, not counted flat:
              `critical` is U+23FA and U+23BF, drawn on EVERY tool call.
              A font missing one is POOR however many others it carries —
              Fira Code has five of nine and neither that matters.
    extras    Ligatures via `calt` — the feature Fira Code, JetBrains Mono
              and Iosevka actually drive => and != through; checking
              `liga` alone reports "none" on all three.

  Browsing installs nothing by looking. Each family is fetched to
  ~/.cache/dotfiles-fonts and previewed there, along with the rows around
  the cursor so the next one has already arrived. You cannot rasterise a
  font you do not have — "preview without downloading" is not a thing —
  but preview without INSTALLING is, and that is this. None of Google's
  monospace families are Nerd-patched, so icons fall back oversized.

  Outside wezterm the specimen and the contact sheet are skipped and you
  get the numbers only: an inline image a terminal cannot draw is not
  ignored, it is printed.
HELP
      ;;
    deps|dependencies)
      # No tool list here on purpose. deps.conf is the manifest and
      # lib/manifest.sh is the only parser; a copy in this file is a second
      # manifest that drifts silently. The previous one had — it listed dotnet
      # (declared nowhere) and omitted qsv (declared), and it printed a
      # `brew install` hint on Linux.
      #
      # `make check` already answers exactly this question. Defer to it.
      if [[ -z "$DOTFILES_ROOT" ]]; then
        echo "Can't locate the dotfiles repo — ~/.bashrc isn't a symlink into it." >&2
        echo "Run 'make check' from your clone." >&2
        return 1
      fi
      make --no-print-directory -C "$DOTFILES_ROOT" check
      echo
      echo "Provenance and drift (declared vs. actual): make -C \"$DOTFILES_ROOT\" status"
      ;;
    *)
      echo "No help found for '$1'. Run 'h' to see all commands."
      ;;
  esac
}
